# Editors, each opened on the same four lines of Jabberwocky, one stanza
# per program.  Every stanza writes the demo file itself and clears the
# screen before the editor starts, so no card leans on another.

shot    ed
cap     GNU ed, the line editor, on a four-line file: print lines 1 to 4,
cap     change one word on line 3 and print the result, then Q to leave
cap     without writing.  It keeps its scratch file on /r0, the RAM disk
cap     most OS-9 systems have, so one is mounted first.
try     ed tmp/EDIT/jabber.txt
run     mkdir -p /dd/tmp/EDIT > /nil 2>/nil; printf "Twas brillig, and the slithy toves\r     Did gyre and gimble in the wabe:\rAll mimsy were the borogoves,\r     And the mome raths outgrabe.\r" > /dd/tmp/EDIT/jabber.txt
wait    2
run     mount -r=256k /r0
wait    3
run     builtin cd /dd
wait    1
run     clear
wait    1
run     ed tmp/EDIT/jabber.txt
wait    4
send    1,4p\r
wait    3
send    3s/mimsy/MIMSY/p\r
wait    3
send    Q\r
wait    2

shot    editor
cap     Started in GAMES: the directory as a lettered list, + and - to page, .
cap     to change directory, and a letter to open that file in umacs.
try     editor
run     builtin cd /dd/GAMES
wait    1
run     clear
wait    1
run     editor
wait    6
snap
send    \003
wait    2

shot    elvis
cap     Elvis, a vi with ex mode, its manual on the disk under DOC/elvis,
cap     opened on a four-line file.  Tildes mark the lines past the end.
try     elvis tmp/EDIT/jabber.txt
run     mkdir -p /dd/tmp/EDIT > /nil 2>/nil; printf "Twas brillig, and the slithy toves\r     Did gyre and gimble in the wabe:\rAll mimsy were the borogoves,\r     And the mome raths outgrabe.\r" > /dd/tmp/EDIT/jabber.txt
wait    2
run     builtin cd /dd
wait    1
run     clear
wait    1
run     elvis tmp/EDIT/jabber.txt
wait    8
snap
send    \033:q!\r
wait    3

shot    elvis_input
cap     Elvis opened already in insert mode, on a new file: what you type
cap     goes straight in, and ESC gets you to the : prompt to write it.
try     elvis_input tmp/EDIT/new.txt
run     mkdir -p /dd/tmp/EDIT > /nil 2>/nil; rm -f /dd/tmp/EDIT/new.txt
wait    2
run     builtin cd /dd
wait    1
run     clear
wait    1
run     elvis_input tmp/EDIT/new.txt
wait    8
keys    Callooh! Callay!
wait    3
snap
send    \033:q!\r
wait    3

shot    elvprsv
cap     Elvis runs this itself when it dies with a file open, to keep the text
cap     for elvrec to bring back; it writes into usr/preserve on /dd.  Run by
cap     hand with nothing to preserve, it says nothing.
try     elvprsv
run     elvprsv
wait    5

shot    elvrec
cap     With nothing preserved: the bare run lists nothing, and asked for a
cap     file it names what it looked for.  It reads the index elvprsv keeps in
cap     usr/preserve on /dd, a directory this disk does not ship; elvprsv
cap     needs it made before it can keep anything.
try     elvrec
run     elvrec
wait    5
run     elvrec jabber.txt
wait    5

shot    em
cap     MicroEMACS 3.8b on a four-line file, with its mode line at the
cap     foot.
try     em tmp/EDIT/jabber.txt
run     mkdir -p /dd/tmp/EDIT > /nil 2>/nil; printf "Twas brillig, and the slithy toves\r     Did gyre and gimble in the wabe:\rAll mimsy were the borogoves,\r     And the mome raths outgrabe.\r" > /dd/tmp/EDIT/jabber.txt
wait    2
run     builtin cd /dd
wait    1
run     clear
wait    1
run     em tmp/EDIT/jabber.txt
wait    8
snap
send    \030\003
wait    3

shot    emacs
cap     Opened on a four-line file: the text, the mode line with the
cap     version and the file's name, and `[Read 4 lines]'. With SYS, where
cap     emacs.hlp is, and USR/LIB/EMACS, where its macros are, on PATH,
cap     ESC ? shows the help.
try     emacs tmp/EDIT/jabber.txt
run     mkdir -p /dd/tmp/EDIT > /nil 2>/nil; printf "Twas brillig, and the slithy toves\r     Did gyre and gimble in the wabe:\rAll mimsy were the borogoves,\r     And the mome raths outgrabe.\r" > /dd/tmp/EDIT/jabber.txt
wait    2
run     builtin cd /dd
wait    1
run     clear
wait    1
run     emacs tmp/EDIT/jabber.txt
wait    8
snap
send    \030\003
wait    3

shot    umacs
cap     The sample poem open, the mode line naming the file and `[Read 4
cap     lines]' below it.
try     umacs tmp/EDIT/jabber.txt
run     mkdir -p /dd/tmp/EDIT > /nil 2>/nil; printf "Twas brillig, and the slithy toves\r     Did gyre and gimble in the wabe:\rAll mimsy were the borogoves,\r     And the mome raths outgrabe.\r" > /dd/tmp/EDIT/jabber.txt
wait    2
run     builtin cd /dd
wait    1
run     clear
wait    1
run     umacs tmp/EDIT/jabber.txt
wait    8
snap
send    \030\003
wait    3

shot    me
cap     MicroEMACS 3.11 with German messages -- Datei for File.
try     me tmp/EDIT/jabber.txt
run     mkdir -p /dd/tmp/EDIT > /nil 2>/nil; printf "Twas brillig, and the slithy toves\r     Did gyre and gimble in the wabe:\rAll mimsy were the borogoves,\r     And the mome raths outgrabe.\r" > /dd/tmp/EDIT/jabber.txt
wait    2
run     builtin cd /dd
wait    1
run     clear
wait    1
run     me tmp/EDIT/jabber.txt
wait    8
snap
send    \030\003
wait    3

shot    mg
cap     The sample poem open, the mode line naming the file and `(Read 4
cap     lines)' below it.
try     mg DOC/samples/jabber.txt
wait    2
run     builtin cd /dd
wait    1
run     clear
wait    1
run     mg DOC/samples/jabber.txt
wait    8
snap
send    \030\003
wait    3

shot    stevie
size    24 80
cap     STEVIE -- "Simply Try this Editor for VI Enthusiasts" -- a small
cap     vi clone: the same movement keys, operators and colon commands.
cap     Here it has the sample poem open, with `~' marking the lines past
cap     the end of the file the way vi does. `:wq' saves and leaves. Its
cap     quick reference is DOC/stevie.
try     stevie poem
run     rm -rf /dd/tmp/STV > /nil 2>/nil; mkdir -p /dd/tmp/STV; cp /dd/DOC/samples/jabber.txt /dd/tmp/STV/poem
wait    4
run     builtin cd /dd/tmp/STV
wait    1
run     clear
wait    1
run     stevie poem
wait    6
snap
send    :q\r
wait    2

shot    hexed
cap     Both halves of its round trip: it writes the file out as a hex dump,
cap     jabber.txt.hd in the directory -t names, and turns the dump back into
cap     the file when the editor -e names has finished.  It hands the editor
cap     to system() as `chd <dir> ; <editor> <file>.hd', a line only
cap     Microware's own shell parses, so with that shell resident and SHELL
cap     naming it, vi opens on the dump in between; at bash the editor step is
cap     skipped, as here.  Without -t the dump goes to /r0, a RAM disk you
cap     mount first.
try     hexed -t=tmp/EDIT -e=vi tmp/EDIT/jabber.txt
run     mkdir -p /dd/tmp/EDIT > /nil 2>/nil
wait    2
run     printf "Twas brillig, and the slithy toves\r     Did gyre and gimble in the wabe:\rAll mimsy were the borogoves,\r     And the mome raths outgrabe.\r" > /dd/tmp/EDIT/jabber.txt
wait    2
run     builtin cd /dd
wait    1
run     clear
wait    1
run     hexed -t=tmp/EDIT -e=vi tmp/EDIT/jabber.txt
wait    10

shot    hexedit
cap     HEXPERT, a hex viewer and editor, on a module from the disk: the
cap     bytes in hex on the left and as text on the right, sixteen to a
cap     row.  The arrow keys page, `e' edits, `q' leaves.
try     hexedit CMDS/today
run     builtin cd /dd
wait    1
run     clear
wait    1
run     hexedit CMDS/today
wait    15
snap
quit    q

shot    pbyte
cap     Three bytes at offset 0 turn `Twas' into `ABCs'. pbyte writes into the
cap     file it is given, so the sample is copied to scratch first, then shown
cap     before and after.
try     cat DOC/samples/jabber.txt > tmp/pb.txt; pbyte tmp/pb.txt 0 41 42 43
run     builtin cd /dd
wait    1
run     clear
wait    1
run     cat DOC/samples/jabber.txt > tmp/pb.txt
wait    3
run     cat tmp/pb.txt
wait    3
run     pbyte tmp/pb.txt 0 41 42 43
wait    5
run     cat tmp/pb.txt
wait    3

shot    chbase
size    40 80
cap     255 into base 16, then into all thirty-five bases at once, 2 to 36,
cap     with a target base of 0.
try     chbase 255 10 16
run     chbase 255 10 16
wait    4
run     chbase 255 10 0
wait    6

shot    sed
cap     sed, the stream editor, substituting on every line of a file.
try     sed "s/the/THE/g" DOC/samples/jabber.txt
wait    2
run     builtin cd /dd
wait    1
run     clear
wait    1
run     sed "s/the/THE/g" DOC/samples/jabber.txt
wait    6

shot    sed_1.06
cap     The same GNU sed 1.06 as sed, its module renamed to match its file
cap     name, doing the same substitution.
try     sed_1.06 "s/the/THE/g" DOC/samples/jabber.txt
wait    2
run     builtin cd /dd
wait    1
run     clear
wait    1
run     sed_1.06 "s/the/THE/g" DOC/samples/jabber.txt
wait    6

shot    view
cap     Elvis opened read-only, which is what the name `view' asks of it:
cap     the status line marks the file read-only.
try     view tmp/EDIT/jabber.txt
run     mkdir -p /dd/tmp/EDIT > /nil 2>/nil; printf "Twas brillig, and the slithy toves\r     Did gyre and gimble in the wabe:\rAll mimsy were the borogoves,\r     And the mome raths outgrabe.\r" > /dd/tmp/EDIT/jabber.txt
wait    2
run     builtin cd /dd
wait    1
run     clear
wait    1
run     view tmp/EDIT/jabber.txt
wait    8
snap
send    :q!\r
wait    3

shot    vi.elvis
cap     Elvis 1.7 kept as vi.elvis in CMDS/REBUILT, because CMDS/vi is PVIC, a
cap     different vi clone; opened here on a four-line file.
try     vi.elvis tmp/EDIT/jabber.txt
run     mkdir -p /dd/tmp/EDIT > /nil 2>/nil; printf "Twas brillig, and the slithy toves\r     Did gyre and gimble in the wabe:\rAll mimsy were the borogoves,\r     And the mome raths outgrabe.\r" > /dd/tmp/EDIT/jabber.txt
wait    2
run     builtin cd /dd
wait    1
run     clear
wait    1
run     vi.elvis tmp/EDIT/jabber.txt
wait    8
snap
send    :q!\r
wait    3

shot    vi_1.0
cap     PVIC 1.0, the Portable VI Clone, in CMDS/REBUILT because the name
cap     `vi' was taken.
try     vi_1.0 tmp/EDIT/jabber.txt
run     mkdir -p /dd/tmp/EDIT > /nil 2>/nil; printf "Twas brillig, and the slithy toves\r     Did gyre and gimble in the wabe:\rAll mimsy were the borogoves,\r     And the mome raths outgrabe.\r" > /dd/tmp/EDIT/jabber.txt
wait    2
run     builtin cd /dd
wait    1
run     clear
wait    1
run     vi_1.0 tmp/EDIT/jabber.txt
wait    8
snap
send    :q!\r
wait    3

shot    vi
cap     PVIC 1.0a, the Portable VI Clone -- this disk's vi, public domain and
cap     needing no Microware module.  A whole-screen editor, so this is the
cap     screen: the whole file at once, with `~' marking every line past the
cap     end of it.  Here it has gone to the last line with `G', opened a new
cap     one with `o' and typed into it -- the change is in the buffer, not
cap     on disk.
cap     To leave: press ESC to get out of insert mode, then type `:q!' and
cap     Enter to quit without saving, or `:wq' to save and quit.
try     vi jabber.txt
# `!' is a history reference to this bash even inside quotes, and the whole
# line then dies with `Event not found' -- the poem below has two of them.
run     set +H
wait    1
run     mkdir -p /dd/tmp/EDIT > /nil 2>/nil
wait    2
run     printf "Twas brillig, and the slithy toves\r     Did gyre and gimble in the wabe:\rAll mimsy were the borogoves,\r     And the mome raths outgrabe.\r\rBeware the Jabberwock, my son!\r     The jaws that bite, the claws that catch!\rBeware the Jubjub bird, and shun\r     The frumious Bandersnatch!\r" > /dd/tmp/EDIT/jabber.txt
wait    3
run     builtin cd /dd/tmp/EDIT
wait    1
run     clear
wait    1
run     vi jabber.txt
wait    8
keys    G
wait    2
keys    o
wait    2
keys    He took his vorpal sword in hand.
wait    2
send    \e
wait    2
snap
send    :q!\r
wait    3
run     builtin cd /dd
wait    1

shot    ctags.elvis
cap     Builds a tags file for elvis from C source: one line per function,
cap     with the file and a search pattern that finds it.
try     ctags.elvis cmdline.c
run     mkdir -p /dd/tmp/EDIT > /nil 2>/nil; rm -f /dd/tmp/EDIT/tags
wait    3
run     cp /dd/SRC/pvic/SRCE/cmdline.c /dd/tmp/EDIT/cmdline.c
wait    6
run     builtin cd /dd/tmp/EDIT
wait    2
run     clear
wait    1
run     ctags.elvis cmdline.c
wait    8
run     cat tags | head -n 8
wait    4
